<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Schönbrunn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Peace of Schönbrunn · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-fifth-coalition">War of the Fifth Coalition</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Schönbrunn was signed at Schönbrunn Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 14 October 1809, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-fifth-coalition">War of the Fifth Coalition</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Austria ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-galicia">West Galicia</a> to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> and other lands to France, which formed them into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a>. Austria lost several million subjects and its access to the sea and paid a heavy indemnity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-schonbrunn.jpg" alt="Treaty of Schönbrunn" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Monnet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trait%C3%A9_de_paix_conclu_%C3%A0_Vienne_le_14_octobre_1809.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a> and Spanish resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> tried to reverse the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a> of 1805 by sparking national uprisings in French-occupied Central Europe, most notably the Tyrolean Rebellion against Napoleon's Bavarian allies. French forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in May 1809, and Austrian troops under Archduke Charles repulsed them at the Battle of Aspern on 21 and 22 May. A few weeks later Napoleon crushed Charles's army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wagram">Battle of Wagram</a> on 5 and 6 July, and the archduke signed the Armistice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/znojmo">Znaim</a> on 12 July. In October Foreign Minister Johann Philipp Stadion was replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Klemens von Metternich</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> imposed harsh terms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> ceded the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-galicia">West Galicia</a> to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, and the Tarnopol district to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. It gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a> and Gradisca, the Imperial Free City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, the March of Istria, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-tyrol">East Tyrol</a> and the Croatian lands southwest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>, which made them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a>, so that Austria lost its access to the Adriatic Sea. Austria recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s earlier conquests and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a> as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, paid a large indemnity and agreed to reduce its army to 150,000 men, a promise it did not keep. It also had to apply Napoleon's Continental System against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>While the negotiations went on, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> narrowly escaped an attempt on his life. On 12 October 1809 Friedrich Staps, a seventeen-year-old son of a Lutheran pastor from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg">Naumburg</a>, was arrested by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-rapp">Jean Rapp</a> and found to carry a large kitchen knife. Asked by Napoleon whether he would be grateful if pardoned, Staps replied that he would kill him none the less. He was shot a few days later, reportedly crying Long live freedom! Long live <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>! After 1813 Staps was seen as a martyr of German nationalism.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, though much weakened, remained a European great power. In 1810 Emperor Francis I married his daughter Marie Louise to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, and under Metternich's changed policy Austrian forces joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-invasion-of-russia">French invasion of Russia</a> in 1812.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Schönbrunn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After defeating Austria, Napoleon took Carniola, western Carinthia, Gorizia, Trieste, Istria and Croatia south of the Sava under the <b>Treaty of Schönbrunn</b> in 1809.</blockquote><a href="/state/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a></li></ul></section>
